# `/target` alone was anchored AND exact, so it covered the default Cargo
# output directory and nothing else. Working in an isolated worktree with a
# per-branch `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` is standing practice here -- sharing one target
# dir across divergent branches produces bogus build failures -- and every such
# directory (`target-allfeat`, `target-<issue>`, whatever the branch is called)
# fell straight through the rule. One that did had 43,807 build artifacts
# staged by an ordinary `git add -A`, including a 723 MiB `libnmp_ffi.a`, and
# GitHub answered the push with HTTP 500 three times before anyone looked past
# "network error". The glob covers the whole family.
#
# The anchor was then its own second incident: `tools/` holds several DETACHED
# Cargo workspaces (nip29-consumer, the three pinned fjall probes plus their
# harness). Each builds its own nested `target/`, which a root-anchored rule
# cannot see, so staging one of those directories with `git add -A` put 99
# build artifacts into a one-line feature-file PR. Same bug class, one
# directory deeper.
/target*
**/target*/
**/*.rs.bk
# Bazel output symlinks (--symlink_prefix=bazel- in .bazelrc). Same discipline
# as /target*: these are per-worktree generated artifacts, never committed.
/bazel-*
__pycache__/
*.pyc
Cargo.lock.orig
.DS_Store
tmp
